.\" -*- nroff -*-
.TH ucc 1 2002-03-24 Exult

.SH NAME
ucc \- UseCode Compiler for Exult

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ucc
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ FILE ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
Games supported by Exult (\fIUltima VII\fR, for example) consist in
large part of Usecode.
.BR exult (1)
basically implements a virtual machine the machine language of which
is Usecode.

.B ucc
is a compiler that targets this machine language.

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h
Display detailed help, including all warning categories, defaults, and examples,
then exit successfully without compiling or writing output.
.TP
.BI \-o " FILE"
Write bytecode to FILE. Compilation errors preserve any previous output.
Without this option, the input extension is replaced by .uco (or .uco is
appended if there is no extension). Standard input defaults to a.ucout.
.TP
.BI \-I " DIRECTORY"
Add an include search directory. May be repeated.
The UCC_INCLUDE environment variable adds one further include directory.
.TP
.B \-s
Select Serpent Isle intrinsics unless overridden by a #game directive.
.TP
.B \-b
Omit the symbol table.
.TP
.BI \-c " MODE"
Use colored diagnostics always, never, or auto (the default).
.TP
.BI \-W NAME
Enable a warning category. Use \-Wno\-NAME to disable it.
Categories are goto, integer\-coercion, shape\-to\-function,
constant\-condition, unreachable\-code, implicit\-return\-type,
implicit\-function\-kind, autonumber, missing\-game, missing\-itemref,
constructor\-arguments, unknown\-directives, unknown\-escape\-sequence,
function\-id\-alias, script\-mismatch, and general.
All categories except function\-id\-alias are enabled by default.
The function\-id\-alias category warns about compatible declarations that
give different names to the same explicit function ID. Both names remain
usable. Incompatible declarations and multiple definitions remain errors.
The script\-mismatch category warns when &, &=, or << combines a value
tracked as a script with a value not tracked as a script.
.TP
.B \-Wall, \-Wno\-all
Enable or disable all warning categories.
.TP
.B \-Werror, \-Wno\-error
Treat enabled warnings as errors, or restore ordinary warning severity.
Use \-Werror=NAME to enable and promote an individual category, and
\-Wno\-error=NAME to exempt it from promotion.
.TP
.B \-u
Disable all warning categories. Hard errors remain enabled.
.TP
.B \-\-
End options, allowing an input filename beginning with a hyphen.

.SH SCRIPT EXPRESSIONS
Arrays and scripts carry compiler metadata with an optional known length.
Assignments propagate these facts; control-flow merges retain a length only
when all incoming paths agree. The new script expression marks a value as a
script. Concatenation with &, &=, or << produces a script when either operand
is a script and warns when only one operand is tracked as a script.
.PP
A repeat command can expand a constructed script:
.nf
var commands = new script { nop; wait 1; };
script item { repeat 3 *commands; }
.fi
Parenthesized operands such as *(first & second) are also supported.
The dereferenced single-count form concatenates the operand with a separate
three-element script tail containing repeat, the negative operand length,
and the count. It uses normal repeat even when syntactically nested.
The operand must be tracked as a script with a known length; otherwise
compilation fails. Dereferencing a script containing a repeat as the body of
another repeat is also an error, since its inner counters would be exhausted
after the first outer iteration. Repeats inside new script use normal repeat
encoding; syntactically nested and explicit two-count repeats retain their
resetting encoding.

.SS Analysis assumptions
The statement assume_script(variable, length); records that a non-class
variable contains a script of the given length. The length must be a
nonnegative compile-time integer constant expression.
.nf
assume_script(animationCommands, 26);
script item { repeat 2 *animationCommands; }
.fi
This is a trusted analysis annotation: it emits no runtime instructions and
does not initialize, convert, or validate the variable's runtime value.
It can refine unknown metadata or mark an ordinary array as a script.
A known scalar value or a different known length is a compilation error.
Known repeats remain tracked, so the assumption cannot bypass the restriction
on dereferencing scripts containing repeats.
The fact follows aliases and normal control-flow merges; reassignment and
calls that can change shared variables invalidate it in the usual way.

.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Diagnostics are written to standard error and include the source filename,
line, byte column, and a source excerpt when available. Warning messages
identify their controlling \-W option. Standard output contains compilation
status messages. The exit status is zero on success and nonzero on failure.
If FILE is omitted, source is read from standard input.
Command-line option and argument errors suggest using \-h for help.

